<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polans (eastern)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polianians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polans were an East Slavic tribe of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, living around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> in the 8th to 10th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> describes them as a peaceful people who paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> before falling under the rule of the Rus' princes. Their land became the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polans-eastern.jpg" alt="Polans (eastern)" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SeikoEn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_Slavic_tribes_peoples_8th_9th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Polans, also known as Polians, Polanians, Polianians or Eastern Polans, were an East Slavic tribe who lived on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> river from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Liubech</a> to Rodnia and down the lower courses of the rivers Ros', Sula, Stuhna, Teteriv, Irpin', <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desna-river">Desna</a> and Pripyat. Their name comes from the Old East Slavic word pole, meaning field, because according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> they lived in the fields. They are distinct from the western Polans of the Early Middle Ages, a West Slavic tribe who were ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. At one stage the Polans were subjugated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The land of the Polans lay at the crossroads of important trade routes and of territories inhabited by other East Slavic tribes, among them the Drevlians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radimichians">Radimichs</a>, Drehovians and Severians, and water routes connected them all. The Road from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, which linked northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, ran along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> through their land. This position allowed the Polans to play an organising role in the consolidation of the East Slavic tribes. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> the tribe was headed by three brothers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyi-shchek-and-khoryv">Kyi, Shchek and Khoryv</a>, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, which became a tribal centre. Two Varangians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/askold-and-dir">Askold and Dir</a>, are considered the first rulers of Kyiv.</p><p>In the 860s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varangians">Varangians</a> arrived and organised several military campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, which eventually defeated them and made peace. From the 9th century the Polans began to be known as Rus', and the region they inhabited as Rus' land, a name they presumably adopted from the Varangians. In the 880s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oleg-of-novgorod">Oleg the Wise</a> conquered the land of the Polans, and from then on their territory became the political, cultural and economic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. In the 10th century the name Polans fell almost out of use, replaced by Rus', and they were last mentioned as a tribe in a chronicle of 944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The chronicles repeatedly note that social and economic relations among the Polans were highly developed compared with those of neighbouring tribes. In the 9th and 10th centuries they practised well-developed arable farming, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> breeding, hunting, fishing and wild-hive beekeeping, along with crafts such as blacksmithing, casting, pottery and goldsmithing. Thousands of kurgans from before the Polan period, found by archaeologists in their region, show that the land could support a relatively dense population. The Polans lived in small families in semi-dug-out earth houses and wore homespun clothes and modest jewellery. Before they converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> they burned their dead and raised kurgan-like embankments over them. According to chronicled legends, their largest cities were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereiaslav</a>, Rodnia, Vyshhorod, Bilhorod Kyivskyi and Kaniv.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polans (eastern)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Oleg and his successors Igor, Olga and Sviatoslav I extended tribute over East Slavic tribes such as the <b>Polianians</b>, Drevlians, Severians and Krivichians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Like the <b>Polans</b> and Vyatichi they paid tribute to the Khazars.</blockquote><a href="/state/severians">Severians</a></li></ul></section>
